    <h2>Day 2: Evening in Gion</h2>

    <p>
      As dusk settled, I walked the stone-paved lanes of Gion. Paper lanterns
      flickered on outside tea houses, and every now and then a figure in full
      kimono would appear around a corner and vanish into a doorway. Most are
      tourists in rentals, but if you're lucky - and quiet - you might catch a
      maiko hurrying to an evening appointment, wooden geta clacking on wet
      stone. I ended the night at a tiny yakitori stall under the train tracks,
      eating skewers of chicken skin and washing them down with cold Asahi. The
      perfect end to 48 hours.
    </p>

